.TH MKPK 1 "2026-07-28" "mkpk 0.2.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
mkpk \- MikroTik PSK port-knock runtime client
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B mkpk knock
.RB ( --invite
.IR FILE " | " --config
.I FILE
.B --client
.IR NAME " [" --router
.IR NAME "]) [" --service
.IR NAME "] [" --check "] [" --debug ]
.br
.B mkpk check
.RB ( --invite
.IR FILE " | " --config
.I FILE
.B --client
.IR NAME " [" --router
.IR NAME "]) [" --service
.IR NAME "] [" --host
.IR HOST "] [" --port
.IR PORT "] [" --json "] [" --debug ]
.br
.B mkpk version
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B mkpk
is the client-side runtime for end users. It performs a staged UDP knock
followed by a short-lived PSK-derived time token; on success the router adds the
observed source address to an allowed address-list for a limited time, which
lets the gated service through. It never contacts the router over SSH \(em
provisioning is a separate concern handled by
.BR mkpk-provision (1).
.PP
Configuration comes either from an invite blob
.RB ( --invite ),
handed to the user out of band, or from a full admin config
.RB ( --config ).
An invite carries only what the client needs: the public router address, the
per-user PSK, and the services and knock ports.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B knock
Send the knock sequence for a service. With
.B --check
it also runs a TCP connect check afterwards.
.TP
.B check
Run only a TCP connect check of the target endpoint, without knocking. Useful
for before/after status. With
.B --json
it prints a machine-readable result.
.TP
.B version
Print the program version and exit.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI --invite " FILE"
Path to an invite blob (prefixed with @) or the raw blob string.
.TP
.BI --config " FILE"
Path to a full admin config (mkpk.yaml).
.TP
.BI --client " NAME"
User name to resolve in the config (with --config).
.TP
.BI --router " NAME"
Router name; optional when the config or invite has a single router.
.TP
.BI --service " NAME"
Service to open; required when more than one is available.
.TP
.BI --host " HOST", \fB--port\fP " PORT"
Override the endpoint the check connects to (default: the service target).
.TP
.B --check
After knocking, verify the target endpoint over TCP.
.TP
.B --json
(check) Emit a JSON result: status (open|closed|error), host, port, attempts,
duration and any error.
.TP
.B --debug
Verbose output: router, bucket, stage ports, local/remote UDP addresses.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.B 0
on success. Non-zero on failure; a failed
.B check
distinguishes a closed/unreachable endpoint via
.BR --json .
.SH EXAMPLES
Knock and verify using an invite:
.PP
.RS
.EX
mkpk knock --invite @laptop.mkpk --service ssh --check
.EE
.RE
.PP
Machine-readable reachability check (for scripts/Ansible):
.PP
.RS
.EX
mkpk check --invite @laptop.mkpk --service ssh --json
.EE
.RE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR mkpk-provision (1)
.SH PROJECT
https://github.com/LazyGatto/mikrotik-psk-knock
